[h1]Platform Efficiency Overlay[/h1]
See what your factory is actually moving, and where it is stuck - no belt readers required.

[h2]The overlay[/h2]
Every machine, belt, space belt and space pipe is coloured by how much of its capacity it is using.

[list]
[*][b]Colour[/b] is capacity used - dark red is stopped, green is keeping up.
[*][b]A green pip[/b] means it is being fed.
[/list]

That second part is what throughput alone cannot tell you. Two belts both at 16% need opposite responses:

[list]
[*]dark [b]with[/b] a pip - fed and still not keeping up, so the problem is here or downstream
[*]dark [b]without[/b] a pip - starved, so the problem is upstream
[/list]

Follow the dark tiles upstream, and the first one you meet with a pip is your bottleneck.

[h2]Platform totals[/h2]
Select a platform to get its total output on the game's own efficiency gauge. Every route off the platform counts toward it - space belt ports, space pipe ports, and items jumping straight to a platform docked alongside. A space belt or pipe runs a dozen lanes in parallel, and all of them are measured as one flow rather than sampling a single lane.

[h2]Using it[/h2]
[list]
[*]Toggle it with the [b]Platform Efficiency[/b] button in the visualization bar, bottom right. It remembers whether it was on.
[*]Nothing is written to your save, so you can remove it at any time.
[*]Requires Shapez Shifter.
[/list]

[h2]Known limits[/h2]
[list]
[*]Machines with speed research read as pinned at 100%.
[*]Fluid moving directly between docked platforms is not measured. Space pipes are.
[*]On a platform mixing belts and pipes, the percentage is right, but the absolute figure adds items and fluid packages together.
[/list]

Source, readme and issue tracker: https://github.com/QuinnBast/Shapez2-Space-Platform-Efficiencies
